# Native inert carrier renderer trial

This staged developer trial renders the proposed native IDL into Rust and TypeScript. The native owner is independently being corrected; this trial does not approve its semantics or install product files. Input copies and their exact origins are in input-pins.json. The externally selected common-control subject supplies unchanged extern types and contracts dependencies.

The generic renderer covers 17 scalar aliases, 50 records (45 structs, four tagged records, one external alias), 23 distinct external types and two protocol frame families. u64/bigint preserves unsigned integers. ByteString owns its data; deserialize_any preserves bytes versus text/array, including through Serde tagged record buffering. Rust nullable required members use explicit presence deserialization; optional members use FieldPresence to preserve absence versus empty/null. TypeScript optional properties require exactOptionalPropertyTypes. Generated auxiliaries reject name collisions. The frame and payload Rust types deliberately have no generic Serialize/Deserialize implementation: M3 must decode by actual frame tag and admitted host selector. TypeScript correlates frame tag/direction/payload in unions; host selector semantics still require admission.

Scalar bounds, numeric constants, NFC, path grammar, order, joins, commitments and frame state are handwritten admission duties, not established by these inert types. Serde is not a CBOR encoder or bounded parser here; serialization via a JSON serializer can produce arrays, so these traits do not establish wire output. M3's dedicated codec must enforce the format, length-before-allocation and canonical output. No generic serde value is authority. No host resource bounds or provider/runtime behavior are qualified.

Run Python -B test_render.py; pinned reference Python can validate inputs/wire-carriers.v1.json against inputs/wire-carriers.meta.schema.json. Run cargo test --locked --offline and strict TypeScript with tsconfig.json. Six Rust tests cover actual representation boundaries; two compile-fail doc tests ensure frames cannot bypass host selection via generic serde. TypeScript probes contain positive and expected-negative assignments. Six Python groups cover repeated generation, namespaces and refusal of ambiguous/unknown constructs. An initial test exposed deserialize_byte_buf accepting JSON strings; the renderer now uses deserialize_any. An initial test incorrectly named an absent ordinal alias; it was corrected to exercise an actual generated record. These are test-development corrections, not historical owner changes.

This file set can be copied and compiled independently with cached pinned Cargo dependencies and the separately pinned TS6 compiler. render.py writes fragments directly without formatting; final product integration must route them through the accepted prettyplease/TS-printer recipe and bind a closed validated IDL input profile, source selection, full generator closure and same eight outputs. No product integration, source-owner acceptance, M1 completion, wire codec/admission, performance, platform or release qualification follows.
